<p>{% person "frank-wetzstein" %}Frank Wetzstein{% endperson %}, {% person "john-kennelly" %}John Kennelly{% endperson %}, and {% person "fred-sommerfeld" %}Fred Sommerfeld{% endperson %}, officers of the {% place "palace-theatre-company" %}Palace Theatre Company{% endplace %}, announced plans for a new Main Street movie theater in March 1936.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandan-theatre/356-1936-architects-sketch.jpg" alt="1936 architect's sketch of the Mandan Theatre">
  <figcaption>Architect's sketch, 1936</figcaption>
</figure></div>

<p>They purchased two downtown lots formerly occupied by {% place "orange-grocery" %}Orange Grocery{% endplace %} and {% place "conyne-jewelry" %}Conyne Jewelry{% endplace %}. Demolition began in April 1936. The new building provided storefronts on both sides of the theater entrance for Conyne Jewelry and {% place "north-dakota-power-and-light-company" %}North Dakota Power and Light Company{% endplace %}, later part of {% place "mdu-resources" %}MDU Resources{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>The south-facing ticket office stood behind a 14-by-40-foot entrance lobby. The 48-by-80-foot auditorium seated 650. Although the rear entrance was at street level, the floor sloped toward the recessed stage, providing an unobstructed view from every seat.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandan-theatre/379-mandantheatre48cropped.jpg" alt="Mandan Theatre on Main Street around 1948">
  <figcaption>Mandan Main Street, c. 1948</figcaption>
</figure></div>

<p>Based on similar Minneapolis-area cinemas, the Mandan Theatre was constructed between April and November 1936. Its opening feature was {% person "shirley-temple" %}Shirley Temple{% endperson %} in <em>Poor Little Rich Girl</em>. A giant neon sign dominated the facade, flashing "Mandan" in black, red, and green with 970 feet of neon tubing and 300 bulbs.</p>

<p>A soundproofed "crying room" in the balcony allowed parents with babies to watch through thick plate-glass windows while listening through a dedicated loudspeaker.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandan-theatre/349-mdn-showboat-theatre.jpg" alt="Showboat Cinema I and II around 1982">
  <figcaption>Showboat Cinema I and II, c. 1982</figcaption>
</figure></div>

<p>After a $100,000 renovation, the building reopened in November 1974 as the 412-seat Showboat Cinema. Two years later, in an attempt to compete with the {% place "kirkwood-plaza-multiplex" %}Kirkwood Plaza Multiplex{% endplace %} and {% place "dakota-theater-bismarck" %}Dakota Theater{% endplace %} multi-screen conversion in nearby Bismarck, an adjacent space became the 144-seat Cinema II, sharing ticketing and concessions with the larger primary theater dubbed "Cinema I".</p>

<p>The Showboat's last film screened in December 1986, and it closed early the next year. Owner {% person "jerry-brekke" %}Jerry Brekke{% endperson %} attributed the decline in patronage to the growth of "at home" VHS movie rentals, the movie studios' reduction of available copies of first-run movies, and rural communities impacted by a depressed farm economy. {% place "mid-continent-theaters" %}Mid-Continent Theaters{% endplace %} leased and remodeled the building, reopening it the following June 1 as the 400-seat Academy Theater. It offered second-run films for 99 cents but closed the following year. Later occupants included a {% place "cicis-pizza-mandan" %}Cici's Pizza{% endplace %} buffet franchise which was open for about a year.</p>

<p>The {% place "mandan-remediation-trust" %}Mandan Remediation Trust{% endplace %} and City of Mandan acquired the building in 2004 as part of a downtown groundwater cleanup. In 2007, the city sought redevelopment proposals. {% person "sandi-mcfall" %}Sandi{% endperson %} and {% person "scott-mcfall" %}Scott McFall{% endperson %} won the competition, purchased the building for $1, and began a $500,000 conversion into a dinner theater. Mysteria Theater opened in December 2008.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandan-theatre/334-mysteria-performance.jpg" alt="Renovated stage at Mysteria Theatre">
  <figcaption>Mysteria Theatre's renovated stage</figcaption>
</figure></div>

<p>The building was rewired, replumbed, and refurbished with new lighting and sound systems. Waxed walls and glass embedded in the black ceiling created a sparkling canopy. Four original Art Deco fixtures lit the main room, which seated 150 to 180 dinner guests. The sloped auditorium floor became level terraces descending to a 30-foot stage and a fiber-optic curtain formerly used at {% place "caesars-palace-las-vegas" %}Caesars Palace{% endplace %} in Las Vegas.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/mandan-theatre/645-mysteria-theatre.jpg" alt="Exterior of Mysteria Theatre"></figure></div>

<p>Mysteria offered dinner and live entertainment, now having a complete kitchen and espresso and traditional bars. After its 2011 sale, the Art Deco building inspired a Roaring Twenties themed bar and restaurant under the name Chicago Place. That business closed in March 2012. The building reopened in 2015 as the Strawberry Bar.</p>
